=== Plane equation and triangular module quotient ===
Work over GF(1000003); F_m=product_(r=1)^m (b-r).
m= 2  delta=dim(B/A)= 1  dim(B/I)= 2  dim((conductor)/I)=0
m= 3  delta=dim(B/A)= 3  dim(B/I)= 6  dim((conductor)/I)=0
m= 4  delta=dim(B/A)= 6  dim(B/I)=12  dim((conductor)/I)=0
m= 5  delta=dim(B/A)=10  dim(B/I)=20  dim((conductor)/I)=0
m= 6  delta=dim(B/A)=15  dim(B/I)=30  dim((conductor)/I)=0
m= 7  delta=dim(B/A)=21  dim(B/I)=42  dim((conductor)/I)=0
m= 8  delta=dim(B/A)=28  dim(B/I)=56  dim((conductor)/I)=0
m= 9  delta=dim(B/A)=36  dim(B/I)=72  dim((conductor)/I)=0
m=10  delta=dim(B/A)=45  dim(B/I)=90  dim((conductor)/I)=0
The audited lattice is B/A=direct_sum_(i=1)^(m-1) k[X]/(X^i).
I=X^(m-1)B has colength m(m-1); the exact annihilator audit finds c/I=0.

=== Nonsquarefree hostile: algebra survives, branch typing does not ===
F=b^m still has conductor b^(m(m-1)) and delta=T_(m-1),
but its tangent cone is the repeated line Y^m; at m=2 this is the cusp k[b^2,b^3], not an ordinary node.

=== Square triangular colengths are exactly a Pell orbit ===
m,q,2m-1 through m<=10000:
  (1,0,1), (2,1,3), (9,6,17), (50,35,99), (289,204,577), (1682,1189,3363), (9801,6930,19601)
Equivalently T_(m-1)=q^2 iff (2m-1)^2-8q^2=1.

=== Degree-(72,108) JC near miss at m=9 ===
delta=36, length(B/c)=72, and degrees (F^8,F^12)=(72,108).
But (F^12)^2=(F^8)^3: the pair is algebraically dependent and has Jacobian zero, not one.
Normalization length, conductor colength, and Keller polynomial degree remain different types.

=== Scope ===
Separable F gives a geometrically ordinary m-fold point; without separability, only the algebraic formulas survive.
The Pell selector preserves the scalar delta only and forgets F, branch labels, incidence, and the conductor ideal.
There is no speed-set/loneliness map and no planar Keller pair; LRC(14) and JC(2) remain open.
